# Where Playwright looks for its browsers, for INSTALLATION as well as for
# running. Both halves matter.
#
# The browsers already exist at /workspace/tools/playwright/browsers, on the
# bind mount, so they survive `stop-dev.ps1`. Without this line the
# `@playwright/test` postinstall would not find them, would download a second
# Chromium into the container filesystem at /root/.cache/ms-playwright, and the
# next `stop-dev.ps1` would destroy it - the same way it used to destroy
# Chromium's apt-installed system libraries. That failure is silent until the
# next run, which then reports `Executable doesn't exist` and reads as a broken
# install rather than a discarded container layer.
#
# It has to be recorded somewhere npm itself reads, not in playwright.config.ts:
# Playwright resolves this path from the environment only, and `npm ci` never
# loads the config file at all.
#
# The key is spelled lowercase-with-underscores rather than as the environment
# variable, because that is npm's own config syntax. npm turns every key in this
# file into `npm_config_<key>` in the environment of lifecycle scripts and of
# anything run through `npm run`, and Playwright's `getFromENV` falls back to
# `npm_config_playwright_browsers_path` when `PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH` is
# unset. That covers `npm ci`, `npm install` and `npm run test:e2e` in one line,
# with no shell-specific `VAR=value` prefix in the scripts - npm prints an
# "unknown user config" warning for it, which is expected and not an error.
#
# Anyone invoking Playwright OUTSIDE npm (a bare `npx playwright test`) is not
# covered by this file and must export PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH themselves.
playwright_browsers_path=/workspace/tools/playwright/browsers
